# Rank 1186 / CVE-2013-4366: Apache HttpComponents HttpClient's fluent
# builder, HttpClientBuilder.build() (httpclient/src/main/java/org/apache/
# http/impl/client/HttpClientBuilder.java), reads its own settable-but-
# optional `hostnameVerifier` field straight into the constructor of the SSL
# socket factory it wires into the default connection manager -- CWE-20.
# `setHostnameVerifier(...)` is the class's only way to populate that field,
# so any caller who builds a client the ordinary way (`HttpClientBuilder.
# create().build()`, never calling that setter) leaves it null. GHSA-pqwh-
# 44jj-p5rm/NVD summarize the impact directly: "does not ensure that
# X509HostnameVerifier is not null." Commit 08140864e3 carries no flag;
# verified against its own diff and the SVN revision it names
# (trunk@1528614) as a real, targeted fix, not a release bump -- it touches
# only HttpClientBuilder.java (plus RELEASE_NOTES.txt) and introduces the
# missing guard: read `this.hostnameVerifier` into a local, and if it is
# null, default it to `SSLConnectionSocketFactory.BROWSER_COMPATIBLE_
# HOSTNAME_VERIFIER` before any of the three branches construct the socket
# factory with it.
#
# Checked why the null actually mattered: at the released 4.3 tag,
# SSLConnectionSocketFactory's own constructor stored the argument verbatim
# (`this.hostnameVerifier = hostnameVerifier;`, no defaulting), and its
# verify path only ran the check when the field was non-null (`if (this.
# hostnameVerifier != null) { this.hostnameVerifier.verify(...); }`, no
# else) -- a null verifier silently skipped hostname verification rather
# than failing closed. A sibling commit (d9e2140, svn r1528447, ~7.5 hours
# earlier) closes that root cause inside SSLConnectionSocketFactory and the
# deprecated SSLSocketFactory directly; 08140864e3 is GHSA's and NVD's named
# fix commit and closes the same gap at HttpClientBuilder's own call site,
# so a caller who reads `this.hostnameVerifier` back out (or a build() that
# never reaches the now-patched constructor) cannot observe the bug either.
#
# vyql match on the vulnerable file labels nothing: no Java binding covers a
# settable-but-unchecked HostnameVerifier passed straight into an SSL
# socket-factory constructor. Checked for reuse first: certValidationDisabled
# 319-321 (vyql/bindings/java/m420f6b67.vyql) already cover setHostnameVerifier
# (true) / setDefaultHostnameVerifier(true) / NoopHostnameVerifier under
# code.CertValidationDisabled (VYQL-CFG-004, CWE-295) -- the same weakness,
# but all three require an explicit disable literal or class name; this shape
# has neither, it is a missing null-check, so nothing existing reused.
#
# Tried a function-scoped presenceNode binding: call_path token for
# "SSLConnectionSocketFactory" present, an identifier token for
# "hostnameVerifier" present, and the guard's own binary expression
# ("hostnameVerifier==null") absent. It labels the vulnerable snippet below
# correctly and clears on the fixed one -- but scanning the real checkout
# with it (`vyql scan --cache off --max-ram 4GB` on 08140864e3^) also fires
# on two call sites that are not this bug: httpclient's own documented
# example (ClientConfiguration.java:145, `X509HostnameVerifier
# hostnameVerifier = new BrowserCompatHostnameVerifier();` -- a freshly
# constructed, never-null verifier passed the same way) and five call sites
# in TestSSLSocketFactory.java, none of which contain the identifier
# "hostnameVerifier" at all -- they match because `node.context.identifier`
# is case-insensitive substring containment over every bare-identifier leaf
# in the function, which includes method and type names, not just variable
# reads: `TestX509HostnameVerifier` (a class name) and
# `getHostnameVerifier()` (a call name) both contain "hostnameVerifier" as a
# substring and light up the same predicate a real field read would. ATTENTION,
# engine gap.
#
# The root cause is the same one already on record for rank 1017 (`java.go`
# emits no `call_arg`/`call_arg_at` token pairing a call with which
# expression fills which argument, unlike c.go/php.go/scala.go/rust.go): with
# that pairing, the binding could ask for `hostnameVerifier` specifically as
# the *argument* of the `SSLConnectionSocketFactory`/`SSLSocketFactory`
# constructor, instead of "this name occurs somewhere, in any role, in this
# function" -- which is what lets a same-named getter, a same-suffixed class,
# and the real vulnerable field read all collapse into one token. Added
# rank 1186 and this spec to that ENGINE-GAPS.md row rather than opening a
# new one -- same limitation, same fix. No definition change shipped.
#
# Full spec suite green. OWASP Java +1.00 (11/11 categories, 0 FP) and Python
# +0.90, both unchanged (no definition touched).

test "rank1186: HttpClientBuilder.build() passes its possibly-null hostnameVerifier straight into the SSL socket factory -- today's scanner labels nothing here"
  lang java
  file HttpClientBuilder.java
  reject VYQL-CFG-004
  code
  ```
  package org.apache.http.impl.client;

  import org.apache.http.conn.HttpClientConnectionManager;
  import org.apache.http.conn.socket.LayeredConnectionSocketFactory;
  import org.apache.http.conn.ssl.SSLConnectionSocketFactory;
  import org.apache.http.conn.ssl.SSLContexts;
  import org.apache.http.conn.ssl.X509HostnameVerifier;

  public class HttpClientBuilder {

      private HttpClientConnectionManager connManager;
      private LayeredConnectionSocketFactory sslSocketFactory;
      private X509HostnameVerifier hostnameVerifier;
      private SSLContext sslcontext;

      public CloseableHttpClient build() {
          HttpClientConnectionManager connManager = this.connManager;
          if (connManager == null) {
              LayeredConnectionSocketFactory sslSocketFactory = this.sslSocketFactory;
              if (sslSocketFactory == null) {
                  if (sslcontext != null) {
                      sslSocketFactory = new SSLConnectionSocketFactory(sslcontext, hostnameVerifier);
                  } else {
                      sslSocketFactory = new SSLConnectionSocketFactory(
                              SSLContexts.createDefault(),
                              hostnameVerifier);
                  }
              }
          }
          return null;
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1186: HttpClientBuilder.build() defaults a null hostnameVerifier to the browser-compatible verifier before use"
  lang java
  file HttpClientBuilder.java
  reject VYQL-CFG-004
  code
  ```
  package org.apache.http.impl.client;

  import org.apache.http.conn.HttpClientConnectionManager;
  import org.apache.http.conn.socket.LayeredConnectionSocketFactory;
  import org.apache.http.conn.ssl.SSLConnectionSocketFactory;
  import org.apache.http.conn.ssl.SSLContexts;
  import org.apache.http.conn.ssl.X509HostnameVerifier;

  public class HttpClientBuilder {

      private HttpClientConnectionManager connManager;
      private LayeredConnectionSocketFactory sslSocketFactory;
      private X509HostnameVerifier hostnameVerifier;
      private SSLContext sslcontext;

      public CloseableHttpClient build() {
          HttpClientConnectionManager connManager = this.connManager;
          if (connManager == null) {
              LayeredConnectionSocketFactory sslSocketFactory = this.sslSocketFactory;
              if (sslSocketFactory == null) {
                  X509HostnameVerifier hostnameVerifier = this.hostnameVerifier;
                  if (hostnameVerifier == null) {
                      hostnameVerifier = SSLConnectionSocketFactory.BROWSER_COMPATIBLE_HOSTNAME_VERIFIER;
                  }
                  if (sslcontext != null) {
                      sslSocketFactory = new SSLConnectionSocketFactory(sslcontext, hostnameVerifier);
                  } else {
                      sslSocketFactory = new SSLConnectionSocketFactory(
                              SSLContexts.createDefault(),
                              hostnameVerifier);
                  }
              }
          }
          return null;
      }
  }
  ```
